articles=[('website-planning','WEBSITES','Before you build: a better website brief','A practical starting point for turning a business goal into a useful website.','6 MIN READ',[
('Start with the job your website needs to do','A website can introduce a business, generate enquiries, sell products, or support existing customers. Pick the primary job first. A clear goal helps you choose content and features without turning every possible idea into a requirement.'),('Know who is visiting','Write down your main audience, what they need to know, and the questions they ask before deciding. Organize the site around their journey, using familiar language and clear navigation.'),('Prepare the essentials','Gather your logo, service descriptions, product information, contact details, and images you have permission to use. Decide who will write and approve content. Content readiness affects both the schedule and the quality of the result.'),('Define what is included','List the pages, forms, integrations, and editing needs. Agree on deliverables, review rounds, responsibilities, and acceptance criteria before work begins. Separate essentials for launch from improvements that can follow.'),('Plan for life after launch','Choose who will update content and handle maintenance. Agree how you will assess whether the website is helping: useful enquiries, completed purchases, or fewer support questions are more informative than a visitor count alone.')]),
('local-search','MARKETING','Make your business easier to find locally','A grounded introduction to local search visibility for service businesses.','5 MIN READ',[
('Make your business information consistent','Use accurate business details wherever you publish them. Your website should clearly explain what you offer, the areas you serve, and how people can contact you. Keep opening hours and public contact information up to date.'),('Build useful service pages','Explain services in the words your customers use. Include practical details, common questions, and relevant local context. Avoid creating many nearly identical location pages with little value for the visitor.'),('Look after your business profile','If your business is eligible for a business listing, provide accurate categories, service information, and authentic imagery. Check the platform’s current eligibility rules before creating or changing a listing.'),('Earn trust through real experiences','Ask customers for honest feedback without scripting their opinion. Publish testimonials only with permission, and respond thoughtfully to questions. Never invent reviews or present concept projects as client work.'),('Review progress with context','Search visibility changes with competition, location, content, and many other factors. Review relevant queries, website visits, and qualified enquiries together. Treat SEO as ongoing improvement, without promising a particular rank or number of leads.')]),
('connected-growth','STRATEGY','Build and grow with one shared plan','Why your website and marketing work better when they start with the same goal.','5 MIN READ',[
('Agree on one audience and one goal','A campaign and its landing page should speak to the same person about the same problem. Define the offer, the intended audience, and the next step before design or advertising begins.'),('Connect the message to the experience','Use consistent language from the campaign through to the website. If an advert promises a specific service, link to a page that explains that service and provides a relevant next step.'),('Make the next step clear','A visitor should understand what will happen after pressing a button or completing a form. Keep the requested information appropriate to the conversation and explain any follow-up process accurately.'),('Plan measurement before launch','Decide what a useful outcome looks like and which tools are appropriate. If tracking is added, plan privacy disclosures and any required consent alongside it. Do not collect information simply because a tool makes it possible.'),('Improve the whole journey','Review campaign quality and website experience together. If enquiries are not relevant, revisit the audience, offer, copy, and form. Test considered improvements and report what actually happened, rather than promising results.')])]
